=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=-2.8,$ $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}=14.24,$ and $n=10.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-10/2}\\,{e}^{-(14.24-2(-2.8)\\theta +10{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{32{\\pi }^{5}}\\,{e}^{\\,-7.12\\,-\\,2.8\\theta \\,-\\,5{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-7.12-2.8\\theta -5{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441782 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441764 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441782 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159918" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441782 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441782 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{12}$ be I.I.D. random variables with probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +2){x}^{-\\theta -3},\\,x\\ge 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{12},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\prod }_{i=1}^{12}{x}_{i}=12.1.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441782 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "64c12c20-f4ed-568b-9dbd-aefd4fca5ee6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441782 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=12$ independent random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=(\\theta +2){x}^{-\\theta -3},\\,x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}((\\theta +2){x}_{i}^{-\\theta -3}) \\\\  & =(\\theta +2)^{n}\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{-\\theta -3} \\\\  & =(\\theta +2)^{n}({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})^{-\\theta -3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are told that ${\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=12.1$ and $n=12.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=(\\theta +2)^{12}(12.1)^{-\\theta -3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441783 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441783 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159877" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441783 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441783 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{8}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{8},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}=1.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for an exponential random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441783 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "722785f1-98e4-52e2-9870-cc5147d770a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441783 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=8$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=1$ and $n=8.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{8}\\,{e}^{-\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441784 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186441772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441784 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159884" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441784 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441784 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ Find the likelihood function of the sample $2.2,\\,1.4,\\,0.7,\\,1.5,\\,2.3,\\,1.7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441784 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d257a6c-5e5c-58a0-af13-fdf60799b0d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441784 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\